1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:07,757
In March 2013, Target opened
its first stores in Canada.

2
00:00:08,293 --> 00:00:13,611
The American retail giant had enormous
ambitions, a recognizable brand, and a plan

3
00:00:13,651 --> 00:00:16,001
to expand at extraordinary speed.

4
00:00:16,539 --> 00:00:21,106
Within nine months, it had opened
one hundred twenty-four locations.

5
00:00:21,146 --> 00:00:25,367
But customers soon encountered empty
shelves, disappointing prices, and missing

6
00:00:25,407 --> 00:00:26,407
products.

7
00:00:26,776 --> 00:00:30,970
Less than two years after entering the
country, Target announced it would close

8
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:32,720
every Canadian store.

9
00:00:33,161 --> 00:00:35,891
The exit produced billions
in accounting charges.

10
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:42,223
How did one of America's most successful
retailers misjudge its expansion so badly?

11
00:00:43,981 --> 00:00:48,614
In the United States, Target had built a
distinctive position between traditional

12
00:00:48,654 --> 00:00:52,264
discount retail and more
fashionable department stores.

13
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:57,793
Its promise combined accessible prices,
appealing design, recognizable private

14
00:00:57,833 --> 00:01:00,723
labels, and a convenient
shopping experience.

15
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:05,599
The red bullseye became one of
America's most familiar retail symbols.

16
00:01:06,197 --> 00:01:08,409
Canadian shoppers knew the brand too.

17
00:01:08,901 --> 00:01:13,041
Many had visited American Target stores
while traveling across the border.

18
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:16,319
That familiarity appeared
to create an opportunity.

19
00:01:16,997 --> 00:01:19,837
Target wasn't introducing
an unknown retailer.

20
00:01:20,347 --> 00:01:24,832
It was bringing an established American
shopping experience to customers who already

21
00:01:24,872 --> 00:01:26,102
had expectations.

22
00:01:28,277 --> 00:01:33,835
In 2011, Target announced an agreement to
acquire leasehold interests in up to two

23
00:01:33,875 --> 00:01:38,035
hundred twenty Zellers store locations from
Hudson's Bay Company for approximately

24
00:01:38,075 --> 00:01:42,585
one point eight three
billion Canadian dollars.

25
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:46,844
The deal provided a rapid
physical entry into Canada.

26
00:01:47,412 --> 00:01:51,786
Rather than developing a small number of
stores and expanding gradually, Target

27
00:01:51,826 --> 00:01:54,596
could establish a nationwide
presence quickly.

28
00:01:55,154 --> 00:01:57,844
But acquiring locations
was only the beginning.

29
00:01:58,397 --> 00:02:03,990
Each site needed conversion, merchandise,
staff, distribution, and technology.

30
00:02:04,715 --> 00:02:09,908
The strategy created enormous opportunity,
alongside an equally enormous execution

31
00:02:09,948 --> 00:02:10,958
challenge.

32
00:02:12,817 --> 00:02:17,248
The first Canadian Target
stores opened in March 2013.

33
00:02:17,288 --> 00:02:21,526
By the end of that year, the company had
opened one hundred twenty-four locations.

34
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:26,808
That pace was remarkable for a retailer
building operations in a new country.

35
00:02:27,261 --> 00:02:33,051
Stores needed working inventory systems,
reliable suppliers, trained employees, and

36
00:02:33,091 --> 00:02:37,261
distribution networks capable of
supporting daily replenishment.

37
00:02:37,748 --> 00:02:42,416
But the rollout schedule left limited room
to resolve problems before they spread

38
00:02:42,456 --> 00:02:43,606
across the network.

39
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:47,225
A mistake affecting one pilot
store could be contained.

40
00:02:47,914 --> 00:02:51,899
A mistake affecting more than a hundred
stores could become a national crisis.

41
00:02:53,846 --> 00:02:56,745
Customers began noticing
a strange contradiction.

42
00:02:57,485 --> 00:03:01,663
Target's stores could look bright and
modern, yet shelves were frequently missing

43
00:03:01,703 --> 00:03:03,793
products shoppers expected to find.

44
00:03:04,279 --> 00:03:08,785
At the same time, distribution centers
were struggling with excess inventory.

45
00:03:09,493 --> 00:03:13,958
Merchandise existed, but it was not
consistently reaching the right shelves in the

46
00:03:13,998 --> 00:03:15,413
right quantities.

47
00:03:15,453 --> 00:03:19,819
This was more serious than a temporary
shortage of popular products.

48
00:03:20,406 --> 00:03:25,089
It suggested problems inside the systems
connecting purchasing, inventory,

49
00:03:25,459 --> 00:03:28,009
warehouses, and individual stores.

50
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:30,832
For customers, the result was simple.

51
00:03:31,304 --> 00:03:34,894
They arrived expecting Target and
left without what they wanted.

52
00:03:36,658 --> 00:03:40,610
Target Canada's inventory
problems began behind the scenes.

53
00:03:41,207 --> 00:03:45,870
The company was implementing new systems
while simultaneously preparing a massive

54
00:03:45,910 --> 00:03:46,940
store launch.

55
00:03:47,466 --> 00:03:50,198
Product records required
extensive information,

56
00:03:50,238 --> 00:03:52,421
and inaccurate data created problems

57
00:03:52,461 --> 00:03:54,509
throughout the supply chain.

58
00:03:54,549 --> 00:03:59,392
Later reporting described serious weaknesses
in the initial information entered into

59
00:03:59,432 --> 00:04:00,432
the system.

60
00:04:00,674 --> 00:04:05,314
When inventory data cannot be trusted,
forecasting and replenishment become

61
00:04:05,354 --> 00:04:06,354
unreliable.

62
00:04:06,975 --> 00:04:12,596
A warehouse may hold merchandise that a
store urgently needs, while the system fails

63
00:04:12,636 --> 00:04:13,966
to direct it correctly.

64
00:04:14,595 --> 00:04:19,045
The company had built physical stores faster
than its operational information could

65
00:04:19,085 --> 00:04:20,595
reliably support them.

66
00:04:22,235 --> 00:04:25,556
The problems extended beyond
individual product records.

67
00:04:26,109 --> 00:04:31,227
Inventory, warehouse, and checkout systems
did not always communicate effectively.

68
00:04:31,763 --> 00:04:36,230
A store might sell one variation of a
product while the replenishment process

69
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:37,420
delivered another.

70
00:04:37,899 --> 00:04:42,122
Meanwhile, distribution centers
accumulated stock that could not be moved

71
00:04:42,162 --> 00:04:43,162
efficiently.

72
00:04:43,458 --> 00:04:45,607
Target faced an expensive contradiction.

73
00:04:46,346 --> 00:04:50,309
Some shelves were empty while other parts
of the supply chain were overloaded.

74
00:04:50,899 --> 00:04:53,288
The failure wasn't simply
a shortage of inventory.

75
00:04:53,984 --> 00:04:58,744
It was a breakdown in knowing what existed,
where it belonged, and when it needed to

76
00:04:58,784 --> 00:04:59,784
move.

77
00:05:01,050 --> 00:05:06,338
The inventory problems damaged the shopping
experience, but Target also faced a

78
00:05:06,378 --> 00:05:07,428
perception gap.

79
00:05:07,862 --> 00:05:12,679
Canadians familiar with American Target
stores expected attractive prices and

80
00:05:12,719 --> 00:05:14,089
distinctive merchandise.

81
00:05:14,666 --> 00:05:18,452
The Canadian stores did not
consistently match those expectations.

82
00:05:19,252 --> 00:05:23,831
Cross-border price differences, different
suppliers, transportation costs, and local

83
00:05:23,871 --> 00:05:26,621
market conditions
complicated the comparison.

84
00:05:27,408 --> 00:05:31,757
But shoppers judged the experience
against the Target they already knew.

85
00:05:31,797 --> 00:05:36,876
When prices disappointed and products were
unavailable, the brand lost two reasons

86
00:05:36,916 --> 00:05:40,966
customers might have chosen it over
established Canadian competitors.

87
00:05:42,953 --> 00:05:47,012
A retailer's first impression matters
because shopping habits are difficult to

88
00:05:47,052 --> 00:05:48,052
change.

89
00:05:48,173 --> 00:05:52,466
Target needed Canadian consumers to
make its stores part of their routines.

90
00:05:53,185 --> 00:05:57,984
Instead, early visitors often encountered
inconsistent merchandise availability.

91
00:05:58,756 --> 00:06:03,623
A customer who cannot find what they need
may return to a familiar competitor.

92
00:06:04,296 --> 00:06:07,495
Repeated disappointments make
that behavior more likely.

93
00:06:08,029 --> 00:06:14,134
By August 2013, Canadian retail reporting
cited a survey in which only twenty-seven

94
00:06:14,174 --> 00:06:18,644
percent of respondents were very
satisfied with their Target experience.

95
00:06:19,271 --> 00:06:23,692
The company had created national awareness
without consistently delivering the

96
00:06:23,732 --> 00:06:25,662
experience that awareness promised.

97
00:06:27,605 --> 00:06:30,015
The financial consequences
appeared quickly.

98
00:06:30,555 --> 00:06:34,978
Target's Canadian business reported
substantial operating losses during its first

99
00:06:35,018 --> 00:06:36,018
year.

100
00:06:36,073 --> 00:06:38,609
By early 2014, cumulative losses had

101
00:06:38,649 --> 00:06:42,083
approached one billion
US dollars, according to

102
00:06:42,123 --> 00:06:43,473
contemporary reporting.

103
00:06:44,213 --> 00:06:49,693
Opening stores, operating distribution
facilities, carrying inventory, and fixing

104
00:06:49,733 --> 00:06:51,863
systems all required money.

105
00:06:52,214 --> 00:06:56,080
Yet the business lacked the sales performance
needed to support that investment.

106
00:06:56,697 --> 00:07:00,320
The company was now facing
two simultaneous challenges.

107
00:07:01,054 --> 00:07:05,198
It needed to repair the customer experience,
and it needed to reduce the financial

108
00:07:05,238 --> 00:07:10,188
burden of a retail network that had expanded
before the business model was stable.

109
00:07:11,939 --> 00:07:16,111
The problems unfolded during a turbulent
period for Target's wider business.

110
00:07:16,738 --> 00:07:20,903
The company was also dealing with the
aftermath of a major US data breach disclosed

111
00:07:20,943 --> 00:07:23,073
in 2013.

112
00:07:23,735 --> 00:07:29,222
In 2014, Target experienced leadership
changes, including the departure of chief

113
00:07:29,262 --> 00:07:33,732
executive Gregg Steinhafel and
changes in Canadian management.

114
00:07:34,412 --> 00:07:38,349
Brian Cornell became Target's
chief executive later that year.

115
00:07:38,833 --> 00:07:43,330
His challenge was to determine whether the
Canadian operation could realistically be

116
00:07:43,370 --> 00:07:44,370
repaired.

117
00:07:44,530 --> 00:07:49,065
The question was no longer whether entering
Canada had been an attractive idea.

118
00:07:49,494 --> 00:07:53,937
It was whether continuing to fund
the expansion made economic sense.

119
00:07:55,749 --> 00:08:00,811
Target Canada worked to improve inventory
availability, operations, and the shopping

120
00:08:00,851 --> 00:08:01,851
experience.

121
00:08:02,402 --> 00:08:07,597
By late 2014, management hoped the holiday
season would demonstrate meaningful

122
00:08:07,637 --> 00:08:08,637
progress.

123
00:08:09,144 --> 00:08:10,998
But the improvement was insufficient.

124
00:08:11,849 --> 00:08:18,159
In January 2015, Brian Cornell explained
that the company could not identify a

125
00:08:18,199 --> 00:08:23,139
realistic scenario in which Target Canada
would become profitable before at least

126
00:08:23,179 --> 00:08:24,179
2021.

127
00:08:24,938 --> 00:08:26,969
That assessment changed the decision.

128
00:08:27,523 --> 00:08:32,437
Continuing to operate meant committing
additional capital to a business with years

129
00:08:32,477 --> 00:08:34,067
of expected losses.

130
00:08:34,567 --> 00:08:39,892
Target now had to choose between financing
a long recovery and accepting an

131
00:08:39,932 --> 00:08:41,062
expensive exit.

132
00:08:42,854 --> 00:08:48,247
On January fifteenth, 2015, Target
announced it would discontinue its Canadian

133
00:08:48,287 --> 00:08:49,287
operations.

134
00:08:49,951 --> 00:08:54,904
Its Canadian subsidiary sought protection
under the Companies' Creditors Arrangement

135
00:08:54,944 --> 00:08:57,534
Act, allowing an orderly wind-down.

136
00:08:57,967 --> 00:09:02,191
At that point, the business operated one
hundred thirty-three stores and employed

137
00:09:02,231 --> 00:09:05,741
approximately seventeen
thousand six hundred people.

138
00:09:06,141 --> 00:09:08,577
Target proposed a
seventy-million-Canadian-dollar

139
00:09:08,617 --> 00:09:09,931
employee trust to provide

140
00:09:09,971 --> 00:09:13,561
compensation and benefits
during the shutdown.

141
00:09:14,501 --> 00:09:18,870
The decision affected thousands of workers,
suppliers, landlords, and communities.

142
00:09:19,566 --> 00:09:24,803
A national expansion that had begun with
enormous expectations was ending less than

143
00:09:24,843 --> 00:09:27,493
two years after its first stores opened.

144
00:09:29,174 --> 00:09:36,187
Target's 2014 financial statements recorded
approximately five point one billion US

145
00:09:36,227 --> 00:09:40,297
dollars in pretax impairment and other
charges associated with leaving Canada.

146
00:09:40,890 --> 00:09:43,979
The figure included major
noncash write-downs.

147
00:09:44,586 --> 00:09:49,868
It was not simply cash lost from operating
stores, and it should not be presented as

148
00:09:49,908 --> 00:09:51,718
a single cash expenditure.

149
00:09:52,308 --> 00:09:56,739
The company also faced costs connected
to liquidation and lease obligations.

150
00:09:57,557 --> 00:10:01,215
By April 2015, the last
Canadian stores had closed.

151
00:10:01,825 --> 00:10:06,944
The expansion had destroyed substantial
value, but the accounting figures tell only

152
00:10:06,984 --> 00:10:08,274
part of the story.

153
00:10:08,675 --> 00:10:14,164
The company had also lost an opportunity
to establish a durable position in Canada.

154
00:10:15,994 --> 00:10:19,104
The evidence reveals four
interconnected decisions.

155
00:10:19,863 --> 00:10:24,732
First, Target expanded at extraordinary
speed before proving its Canadian operating

156
00:10:24,772 --> 00:10:25,772
model.

157
00:10:26,024 --> 00:10:30,798
Second, it relied on new inventory and
distribution systems that were not

158
00:10:30,838 --> 00:10:32,388
sufficiently dependable.

159
00:10:32,785 --> 00:10:38,064
Third, it underestimated the gap between
Canadian customers' expectations and the

160
00:10:38,104 --> 00:10:39,914
experience stores could deliver.

161
00:10:40,388 --> 00:10:45,122
And fourth, the size of the rollout
made correcting mistakes expensive and

162
00:10:45,162 --> 00:10:46,162
difficult.

163
00:10:46,375 --> 00:10:48,589
These problems reinforced one another.

164
00:10:48,995 --> 00:10:52,171
Poor availability
weakened customer demand.

165
00:10:52,750 --> 00:10:54,705
Weak demand increased losses.

166
00:10:55,348 --> 00:10:59,467
And mounting losses reduced the
time available to fix operations.

167
00:11:01,370 --> 00:11:05,763
Target didn't fail in Canada because
Canadians had never heard of the brand.

168
00:11:06,322 --> 00:11:10,782
It failed to consistently deliver the
experience that made the brand attractive in

169
00:11:10,822 --> 00:11:11,822
the first place.

170
00:11:12,214 --> 00:11:16,629
Management committed to national scale
before the underlying operation was ready.

171
00:11:17,066 --> 00:11:21,070
By the time the problems were fully visible,
the company was carrying the costs of

172
00:11:21,110 --> 00:11:22,840
more than a hundred stores.

173
00:11:23,470 --> 00:11:27,487
The lesson is not that international
expansion is inherently dangerous.

174
00:11:28,096 --> 00:11:32,148
It is that brand recognition cannot
replace operational readiness.

175
00:11:32,895 --> 00:11:36,890
A successful formula does not
automatically survive a new market.

176
00:11:37,327 --> 00:11:39,117
And every brand leaves evidence.
